module

public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.FreeModule.Basic
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Finiteness

/-!
# Strictly positive integral grading of a sharp lattice monoid

Source: `literature/illusie-laszlo-orgogozo-travaux-de-gabber_arXiv-1207.3648.pdf`,
VI 1.6, printed p. 70, and VIII 3.1.1, 3.1.10, printed pp. 98–99, for sharp fs monoids.
The lattice-grading assertion and proof are an agent-supplied convex-algebra refinement;
VIII Remark 3.1.29, printed p. 103, is contextual, not a strict-separation theorem.
(blueprint: lem-trf-sharp-monoid-positive-grading)
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

@[expose] public section

universe u

/-- A finitely generated sharp submonoid of a finite-rank free abelian group admits a
strictly positive integral grading, whose restriction takes values in the natural numbers.

Source: the agent-supplied convex-algebra refinement for ILO, *Travaux de Gabber*,
VI 1.6 and VIII 3.1.1, 3.1.10; VIII Remark 3.1.29 is motivating context only.
(blueprint: lem-trf-sharp-monoid-positive-grading)

The explicit basis `e : Module.Basis (Fin r) ℤ G` states the finite-rank hypothesis.
Neither `r` nor `P` is required to be nonzero; the basis and finite generating set may be empty.

Proof (the four steps of the DAG node):
1. Embed `G` in its rational scalar extension using the finite basis, and let `C` be the
   rational cone generated by a finite generating list for `P`. If both `x` and `-x` belong
   to `C`, clear denominators with a common positive integer `N` to obtain `a, b ∈ P`
   with `Nx = a ⊗ 1` and `-Nx = b ⊗ 1`. Injectivity of the lattice embedding gives
   `a + b = 0`. Sharpness gives `a = b = 0`, hence `x = 0` and `C` is pointed.
2. Apply `lem-trf-rational-cone-dual-finite` for finitely many rational forms whose
   nonnegative locus is `C`. Their sum is nonnegative on `C`. If it vanishes at `c ∈ C`,
   every summand vanishes, so `-c ∈ C` as well; pointedness gives `c = 0`.
   Thus their sum is strictly positive on every nonzero cone element. An empty family
   forces `C` to be the whole vector space, which pointedness forces to be zero.
3. Multiply this rational functional by a common positive denominator of its values on
   the finite basis. Its values on `G` are then integers, defining `w : G →+ ℤ`.
   The lattice embedding and positive denominator preserve strict positivity on nonzero `P`.
4. Each value of `w` on `P` is nonnegative: it is zero at zero and positive elsewhere.
   Its restriction therefore gives `wP : P →+ ℕ`, with the displayed integer-cast equality.
   For `P = {0}` the positivity condition is empty and the zero maps suffice.
-/
theorem exists_strictlyPositiveIntegralGrading
    (G : Type u) [AddCommGroup G] [Module.Free ℤ G]
    (r : ℕ) (e : Module.Basis (Fin r) ℤ G)
    (P : AddSubmonoid G) (hP : P.FG)
    (hsharp : ∀ p : G, p ∈ P → -p ∈ P → p = 0) :
    ∃ (w : G →+ ℤ) (wP : P →+ ℕ),
      (∀ p : G, p ∈ P → p ≠ 0 → 0 < w p) ∧
      (∀ p : P, (wP p : ℤ) = w p) := by
  sorry

end
